### Docker Image vs Container

**Docker Image:**
A Docker image is a **read-only template** containing the application, dependencies, libraries and required filesystem content.

**Container:**
A container is a **runtime instance created from an image**. It can be running, stopped, paused or removed.

Your notes already capture the key relationship:

```text
Docker Image
     ↓
  Container
```

Multiple containers can be created from the same image. 

### Interview explanation

> "A Docker image is a reusable, read-only template, while a container is an instance of that image running as a process. The same image can be used to create multiple containers."

### Commands to remember

```bash
docker images
docker ps
docker ps -a

docker run -d --name nginx-container nginx

docker stop <container>
docker start <container>
docker restart <container>
docker pause <container>
docker unpause <container>
docker kill <container>
docker rm <container>
```

These are the lifecycle commands  practiced. 

